.TH CSCX 1 "2026-08-31" "cscx 0.13.0" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
cscx \- convert terminal color schemes between formats and into editor themes
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cscx convert
.I FILE
.B \-\-to
.I TARGET
.RB [ \-\-from
.IR FORMAT ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-fill ]
.RB [ \-\-terminal\-exact ]
.RB [ \-\-contrast
.IR RATIO ]
.RB [ \-\-name
.IR NAME ]
.br
.B cscx parse
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-from
.IR FORMAT ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-color ]
.br
.B cscx detect
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B cscx activate
.I FILE
.B \-\-for
.I APP
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.RB [ \-\-name
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-profile
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-backup ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-validate ]
.br
.B cscx live
.RI [ FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-reset ]
.br
.B cscx browse
.RB [ \-p
.IR PATH ]
.br
.B cscx preview
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-from
.IR FORMAT ]
.RB [ \-\-width
.IR COLUMNS ]
.br
.B cscx list
.RI [ QUERY ...]
.RB [ \-p
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B cscx nvim\-themes
.RB [ \-\-list ]
.RB [ \-\-only
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR DIR ]
.br
.B cscx active
.RB [ \-\-no\-editors ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B cscx formats
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cscx
reads a terminal color scheme in any supported format into a single canonical
palette, and writes that palette back out in any other format. Nine terminal
formats are read and written; three editors are written only.
.PP
The design is hub\-and\-spoke. Every format is parsed into one
.IR Palette ,
and every format is emitted from it, so support costs one parser and one
emitter per format rather than a converter for each ordered pair.
.PP
Two rules govern the output. Nothing is invented: a value the source did not
state is left out rather than guessed, and
.B \-\-fill
is how you ask for conventional defaults. Nothing is silently altered: a value
either survives a conversion unchanged or is absent because the target format
cannot express it.
.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI convert " FILE"
Read
.I FILE
and write it in the format given by
.BR \-\-to .
Output goes to standard output unless
.B \-o
is given.
.TP
.BI parse " FILE"
Read
.I FILE
and show the resulting palette, with terminal color swatches when standard
output is a terminal. Values the source did not set are reported on standard
error.
.TP
.BI detect " FILE"
Report which formats
.I FILE
resembles, most confident first, with a score from 0.0 to 1.0. Useful when
.B convert
guesses wrong and you need a name for
.BR \-\-from .
.TP
.BI activate " FILE"
Install a scheme into an application's configuration so it persists. Writes
the theme, points the application's config at it, and reports how to reload.
See
.B ACTIVATION
below; this is the only command that edits a file you did not ask it to
create.
.TP
.BI live " \fR[\fPFILE\fR]\fP"
Recolour the running terminal immediately, without changing any
configuration. The change lasts until
.B \-\-reset
or until the terminal exits.
.TP
.B browse
Open the terminal browser: every scheme found on this machine in a list, a
preview of the highlighted one beside it, and a key to write it out in another
format. See
.B BROWSING
below.
.TP
.BI preview " FILE"
Print one scheme as colors \(em its palette, a shell session and a syntax
sample \(em without opening the browser.
.TP
.BI list " \fR[\fPQUERY...\fR]\fP"
List the schemes found on this machine, optionally narrowed by
.IR QUERY .
See
.B FILTERING
below.
.TP
.B nvim\-themes
Read neovim's colorschemes into palettes and cache them, so they can be
browsed, previewed and converted like any other scheme. See
.B NEOVIM COLORSCHEMES
below.
.TP
.B active
Show which scheme each application is currently using. See
.B DETECTING THE ACTIVE SCHEME
below.
.TP
.B formats
List every readable format and writable editor, with file extensions, accepted
aliases, and where a generated editor theme belongs.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-t " TARGET" "\fR, \fP" \-\-to " TARGET"
Output format or editor. The literal
.B all
writes every supported target and requires
.B \-o
to name a directory. Required for
.BR convert .
.TP
.BI \-f " FORMAT" "\fR, \fP" \-\-from " FORMAT"
Parse as
.I FORMAT
instead of detecting it. Editors cannot be read, so they are not accepted here.
.TP
.BI \-o " PATH" "\fR, \fP" \-\-output " PATH"
Write to
.IR PATH .
With
.BR "\-\-to all" ,
.I PATH
is a directory, created if needed.
.TP
.B \-\-fill
Derive values the source omitted, and mark each derived line as such. See
.B GAP FILLING
below. Without this flag the output carries only what was read.
.TP
.B \-\-terminal\-exact
Editors only. Build the theme from the sixteen palette colors alone, deriving
no intermediate shades. Gives exact parity with the terminal at the cost of a
flatter interface; where that costs something visible, the generated file says
so in a comment.
.TP
.BI \-\-contrast " RATIO"
Editors only. Minimum contrast ratio for comments and line numbers against the
background. Defaults to 4.5, the WCAG AA threshold for normal text. A value of
.B 0
disables the check.
.TP
.BI \-\-name " NAME"
Override the scheme name. Otherwise it comes from the source file, or from a
name the format records itself, such as konsole's
.I Description
field.
.TP
.BI \-a " APP" "\fR, \fP" \-\-for " APP"
The application to configure. Required by
.BR activate .
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print what would change and stop. Nothing is written.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not ask before editing an existing file.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " PATH"
konsole only. The
.I .profile
to point at the installed scheme. Without it the scheme is installed but not
selected, and
.B cscx
says so.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-backup
Do not copy existing files aside before editing them. Rolling back a rejected
result then becomes impossible.
.TP
.B \-\-reset
.B live
only. Restore the terminal's own configured colors.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-validate
Skip asking the application whether it accepts the result.
.TP
.BI \-p " PATH" "\fR, \fP" \-\-path " PATH"
Search this file or directory in addition to the usual locations. A directory
is scanned one level deep. Repeatable. Accepted by
.B browse
and
.BR list .
.TP
.B \-\-list
.B nvim\-themes
only. List the colorschemes neovim can load, without reading any of them.
.TP
.BI \-\-only " NAME"
.B nvim\-themes
only. Read just this colorscheme. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-editors
.B active
only. Skip the editors, which have to be started in order to be asked.
.TP
.BI \-\-width " COLUMNS"
Width of the
.B preview
panels. Defaults to 76.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit machine\-readable output.
.RB For " parse" ,
the palette; for
.BR detect ,
the ranked formats; for
.BR list ,
the discovered schemes.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Suppress the color swatches in
.B parse
output.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.
.SH TERMINAL FORMATS
All nine are both read and written.
.TP
.B alacritty
.IR .toml ", " .yml .
Both the current TOML format and the YAML used before 0.13.
.TP
.B foot
.IR foot.ini .
Regular, bright and dim slots, alpha, and the two\-value cursor.
.TP
.B ghostty
.IR config .
The
.BI palette " N" =#hex
form and kebab\-case keys.
.TP
.B iterm2
.IR .itermcolors .
Floating\-point components; XML and binary property lists are both read, XML
is written.
.TP
.B kitty
.IR kitty.conf .
256 slots, tab bar colors, and symbolic references such as
.BR "cursor_text_color background" .
.TP
.B konsole
.IR .colorscheme .
Intense and faint variants, description, opacity. Aliased as
.BR kterminal .
.TP
.B wezterm
.IR .toml .
The
.I ansi
and
.I brights
arrays and the
.I [metadata]
name.
.TP
.B windows\-terminal
.IR .json .
A bare scheme object, an array of them, or one nested in
.IR settings.json .
Aliased as
.BR wt .
.TP
.B xresources
.IR .Xresources .
Recognises urxvt and xterm prefixes and X11
.BI rgb: rr / gg / bb
values. Aliased as
.BR xrdb .
.PP
Color values are read in every spelling these formats use:
.BR #rgb ,
.BR #rrggbb ,
.BR #rrggbbaa ,
.BR 0xrrggbb ,
bare
.BR rrggbb ,
X11
.BR rgb: rr / gg / bb ,
CSS
.BR rgb() ,
decimal
.B r,g,b
triples, and CSS or X11 color names.
.
.SH EDITORS
Editors are written, not read. A terminal scheme holds around twenty values
while an editor theme holds hundreds of semantic highlight groups, so the
mapping is lossy in a way that cannot be reversed.
.TP
.B vim
.IR ~/.vim/colors/ NAME .vim
.TP
.B neovim
.IR ~/.config/nvim/colors/ NAME .lua .
Covers core groups, treesitter captures, LSP semantic tokens and diagnostics.
Aliased as
.BR nvim .
.TP
.B helix
.IR ~/.config/helix/themes/ NAME .toml .
Names its colors in a
.I [palette]
table and refers to them by role. Aliased as
.BR hx .
.TP
.B emacs
.IR ~/.emacs.d/themes/ NAME \-theme.el .
Built\-in faces only; package faces would be guesswork about what is
installed. Also sets
.IR ansi\-color\-names\-vector ,
so shell and compilation buffers match the source terminal.
.TP
.B vscode
.IR <extension>/themes/ NAME \-color\-theme.json .
Sets the integrated terminal's sixteen colors as well as the editor's. Aliased
as
.BR code .
.TP
.B claude\-code
.IR ~/.claude/themes/ NAME .json .
See
.B CLAUDE CODE
below. Aliased as
.B claude
and
.BR cc .
.TP
.B cursor
.TQ
.B antigravity
The same theme file as
.BR vscode ,
byte for byte; only the extension directory differs, under
.I ~/.cursor
and
.I ~/.antigravity
respectively.
.BR antigravity " is aliased as " ag .
.PP
The mapping is base16: ANSI red becomes the variable and diff\-deleted role,
blue becomes functions, magenta becomes keywords, and so on.
.PP
base16 also has a greyscale ramp \(em statusline, cursorline, line numbers,
comments \(em that no terminal palette contains. Those shades are interpolated
from the background toward the foreground in the OKLab color space, which
steps evenly to the eye where a linear\-light blend visibly overshoots.
.B \-\-terminal\-exact
suppresses this and uses palette colors only.
.PP
Comments are held to the contrast ratio set by
.BR \-\-contrast ,
and the blend is capped at 85% of the way to the foreground. The cap matters
for low\-contrast schemes, where reaching the target would put comments on top
of normal text; when it binds, the generated theme records the shortfall in a
comment.
.PP
Themes carry terminal color indices alongside their true\-color values, so an
editor running without true\-color support still renders correctly. A color
that is one of the scheme's own ANSI slots is written as index 0 to 15, so the
terminal draws it from the same palette the theme came from. The vim and
neovim themes also set the built\-in terminal's colors.
.PP
An editor theme needs a complete palette. Missing values that
.B \-\-fill
can supply prompt for it; a missing hue \(em one of the eight normal ANSI
colors \(em is refused outright, because an absent hue cannot be derived from
the others.
.SS Packaging a VS Code theme
.B vscode ,
.B cursor
and
.B antigravity
emit the theme document itself, which those editors load from within an
extension rather than on its own. The smallest wrapper is a directory holding
the generated file under
.I themes/
and a
.I package.json
naming it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
{
  "name": "mine", "version": "1.0.0", "engines": { "vscode": "*" },
  "contributes": { "themes": [ {
    "label": "Mine", "uiTheme": "vs\-dark",
    "path": "./themes/mine\-color\-theme.json"
  } ] }
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use
.B vs\-light
as the
.I uiTheme
for a light scheme. Drop that directory into
.IR ~/.vscode/extensions ,
.I ~/.cursor/extensions
or
.I ~/.antigravity/extensions
and restart the editor.
.
.SH BROWSING
.B cscx browse
opens a terminal interface over the schemes already installed: the list on the
left, a preview of the highlighted scheme on the right, and the path it was
found at along the bottom.
.PP
The preview is faithful for terminals and approximate for editors. A terminal
scheme is sixteen colors plus foreground and background, so the simulated
shell session shows exactly what the real one will look like. The syntax
sample is painted with the base16 roles the editor writers assign, which shows
the mapping rather than any editor's own rendering.
.SS Navigation
Arrow keys work throughout. The vim keys work alongside them:
.B j
and
.B k
move down and up,
.B g
and
.B G
jump to the first and last entry, and
.B "ctrl+d" / "ctrl+u"
move by ten.
.PP
.B l
moves right into the preview and
.B h
moves back to the list.
.B j
and
.B k
scroll whichever of the two has focus, so
.B l
followed by
.B j
scrolls the preview rather than quietly moving the selection behind it.
.PP
None of these apply while the filter has focus, where they are simply typed.
.SS Keys
.TP
.BR ? " or " F1
Show a summary of these keys, the filter syntax, and what the columns in the
list mean. Any of
.BR esc ,
.BR ? ,
.B F1
or
.B q
closes it again.
.TP
.B /
Focus the filter. See
.B FILTERING
for what it accepts.
.TP
.B a
Apply the highlighted scheme to the running terminal. Nothing is written to
disk. Quitting restores the terminal's own colors, as does
.BR u .
.TP
.B u
Undo a live preview.
.TP
.B A
Activate the highlighted scheme for an application. The plan is shown before
anything happens; Enter applies it, Escape cancels.
.TP
.B c
Copy the highlighted scheme to another format. Pick a target, confirm the
destination, and it is written. Editing the destination keeps it: changing the
target afterwards re\-suggests a filename only while the field still holds the
previous suggestion.
.TP
.B r
Rescan. Anything written to
.I ~/.config/cscx/themes
appears in the list, because that directory is one of the searched locations.
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.SS What it does not do
Browsing never touches a live configuration. Copying writes a new file and
reports where it went; pointing an application at that file, and reloading the
application, remain yours to do.
.SS Searched locations
The usual theme directories for each supported application \(em among them
.IR ~/.config/kitty ,
.IR ~/.config/alacritty/themes ,
.IR ~/.config/ghostty/themes ,
.IR ~/.local/share/konsole ,
.I /usr/share/konsole
and
.I ~/.config/foot/themes
\(em plus anything given with
.BR \-\-path .
The search is an explicit table rather than a sweep of the home directory:
several formats have no distinctive extension, so a broad walk would mean
sniffing thousands of unrelated files.
.PP
.B browse
needs Textual, which is an optional dependency:
.PP
.RS
.EX
pip install \(aqcscx[tui]\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Everything else in
.B cscx
has no dependencies at all.
.
.SH ACTIVATION
.B cscx activate
is the only command that edits configuration you did not ask it to create, so
it works in stages that can each be inspected or undone:
.PP
.RS
plan \(-> back up \(-> apply \(-> validate \(-> roll back on failure
.RE
.PP
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints the plan and stops. Without it the plan is printed anyway, and any file
that already exists is confirmed before being touched unless
.B \-\-yes
is given.
.PP
Existing files are copied aside first, to
.IR NAME .cscx\- TIMESTAMP .bak\fR.
A backup is never overwritten: two activations inside the same second get
distinct names, because the older copy is the one holding the untouched
original.
.PP
Edits are anchored to a marker comment, so activating a second time rewrites
one line rather than appending another include. Alacritty is the exception:
its
.I import
must sit inside the
.I [general]
table, and a second
.I [general]
would be invalid TOML rather than merely untidy, so that file is edited
structurally instead.
.PP
Where the format can be machine\-checked the result is validated by the
application itself \(em alacritty's TOML is re\-parsed,
.B foot \-\-check\-config
and kitty's own loader are run when those programs are installed. If the
application would have rejected the result, every backup is restored, every
newly created file is removed, and the command fails without having changed
anything.
.SS Applications
.B kitty ,
.B alacritty ,
.B foot ,
.B ghostty
and
.B konsole
get a theme file plus a config edit.
.PP
Editors only ever get a file placed where they already look. Nothing is added
to your
.IR init.vim ,
.I init.lua
or
.IR init.el ,
because choosing a colorscheme should stay a deliberate act; the command
prints the one line to run. For VS Code and its forks the wrapping extension
directory is generated too, since a theme document alone is not loadable.
.PP
konsole installs the scheme but cannot select it without knowing which profile
you use; pass
.B \-\-profile
or pick the scheme in its settings.
.
.SH LIVE PREVIEW
.B cscx live
sets the terminal's colour registers with OSC escape sequences. It changes no
file, needs nothing enabled first, and every terminal this project targets
understands it \(em unlike a remote\-control protocol, which is per\-terminal
and off by default.
.PP
The sequences go to
.I /dev/tty
rather than standard output, so they work when output is piped and do not
disturb a full\-screen program that owns the terminal. Inside
.B tmux
they are wrapped in its passthrough form, which tmux would otherwise swallow.
.PP
.B cscx live \-\-reset
restores the terminal's configured colors. The browser does this for you when
you quit.
.
.SH DETECTING THE ACTIVE SCHEME
.B cscx active
answers two different questions that share a name.
.PP
Terminals have no way to be asked, so their configuration is read and the
answer resolved to an actual file \(em which means it can be parsed and shown
as colors. Order is what decides: kitty and foot apply directives top to
bottom, so a configuration holding both inline colors and an
.I include
has a winner that a plain search for
.I include
reports wrongly.
.B cscx
follows the ordering and says which one wins.
.PP
Editors are asked directly, because an init file can set a colorscheme
conditionally or through a plugin and only the editor knows the outcome. What
comes back is a name and nothing more: an editor theme cannot be read back
into sixteen ANSI slots, which is the same reason editors are written but
never read elsewhere in
.BR cscx .
Starting vim and neovim is the slow part, so
.B \-\-no\-editors
skips it.
.PP
Some answers are genuinely ambiguous and are reported that way rather than
guessed. konsole stores its scheme per profile, so a machine with several
profiles has several answers, and unless
.I konsolerc
names a default there is no way to know which applies.
.PP
In
.B cscx browse
the schemes a terminal is currently using are marked in the list.
.
.SH FILTERING
The filter in
.B browse
and the optional query to
.B list
work the same way.
.PP
A bare word is matched fuzzily against the scheme's name, its source, its
format and where it was found, all together. The match is a subsequence, so
.I b16sulph
finds
.IR base16\-atelier\-sulphurpool ,
and results are ranked: an exact substring outranks a scattered match, a match
that starts a word outranks one in the middle, and adjacent characters outrank
spread\-out ones.
.PP
Several bare words all have to match, which is what makes
.I gruv dark
narrower than either word alone.
.PP
A word of the form
.BI source: VALUE
constrains that field exactly instead of fuzzily.
.B format:
and
.B origin:
do the same for those fields, and
.B src:
and
.B fmt:
are accepted as short forms. Constraints and bare words combine, so
.I source:neovim gruv
is every gruvbox variant that came out of neovim.
.PP
A prefix that is not one of those fields is treated as ordinary text, not as a
failed constraint.
.SS Source is not format
The
.B source
is the application a scheme came from; the
.B format
is how it is written. They usually agree, but not always: neovim colorschemes
are cached in kitty's format, so their source is
.I neovim
and their format is
.IR kitty .
Listings show the source, because calling them kitty would be misleading.
.
.SH CLAUDE CODE
Claude Code is themable. It ships six presets \(em
.BR dark ,
.BR light ,
.BR dark\-ansi ,
.BR light\-ansi ,
.B dark\-daltonized
and
.B light\-daltonized
\(em and reads custom themes from
.IR ~/.claude/themes/ SLUG .json\fR,
selected with
.BI theme:\  custom: SLUG
in
.IR ~/.claude/settings.json ,
or from its
.B /theme
picker.
.PP
Before generating anything, consider whether you need to. The
.B dark\-ansi
and
.B light\-ansi
presets already render the whole interface from the terminal's sixteen ANSI
colors, so theming the terminal themes Claude Code too, permanently and with
nothing to keep in step.
.PP
The
.B claude\-code
target is for the other case: dressing Claude Code in a scheme that is not the
terminal's current one. It writes all 72 tokens with a base chosen from the
palette's lightness.
.SS Why the token names matter
Claude Code merges a custom theme by walking the overrides and keeping only
those whose token the base preset already has, and whose value is a valid
color. Anything else is dropped without a message, so a mistaken token name
yields a theme that loads and quietly does nothing.
.PP
The token names and their mapping to ANSI colors are therefore not invented
here. They are read out of the
.B dark\-ansi
and
.B light\-ansi
presets in the installed Claude Code, which differ from one another on 37 of
the 72 tokens. A test checks the pinned list against whichever build
.B claude
currently runs, because the set does change between releases: 2.1.76 had 67
tokens and called one
.IR selectionBackground ,
which by 2.1.221 was 72 and
.IR selectionBg .
.PP
Where the scheme carries something the sixteen ANSI slots cannot express \(em
its foreground, background and selection \(em that value is used in
preference.
.B \-\-terminal\-exact
restricts the output to the ANSI slots instead.
.
.SH NEOVIM COLORSCHEMES
Editors are written and not read, because a colorscheme is a program rather
than a table of colors and parsing one is hopeless. Neovim, though, can be
asked: it will load a colorscheme and report what it resolved to, which is
both easier and more reliable than parsing would have been.
.PP
.B cscx nvim\-themes
loads every colorscheme neovim can find \(em plugins included \(em and writes
each one out as a palette under
.IR ~/.cache/cscx/nvim ,
in kitty's format because it holds a palette losslessly and everything else in
.B cscx
already reads it. They then appear in
.BR browse ,
.B list
and
.B convert
with no special handling.
.PP
Every scheme is loaded inside a single neovim process. Starting one per scheme
would turn about ten seconds into several minutes.
.SS Exact and derived
Two tiers, and which one applied is written into each file rather than
implied.
.TP
.B exact
The colorscheme sets
.IR terminal_color_0 .. 15 ,
so the sixteen ANSI colors come from its author unchanged. This is the common
case: 169 of 179 colorschemes on the machine this was developed on.
.TP
.B derived
It does not, so the palette is inferred from semantic highlight groups \(em
.I String
is green,
.I Function
is blue,
.I Keyword
is magenta. That is the inverse of the base16 mapping used to write editor
themes, and it is an approximation.
.PP
A scheme too monochrome to derive a palette from is skipped rather than
guessed at.
.PP
.B exact
means the colors the colorscheme configures, which is not always the
conventional ANSI arrangement: some schemes put an unexpected hue in
.IR terminal_color_1 .
That is faithfully what neovim's built\-in terminal will use.
.
.SH GAP FILLING
By default the output carries only what was read, leaving the target program
to apply its own defaults. Converting a konsole scheme, which has no cursor
color, to kitty produces a config with no cursor line.
.PP
.B \-\-fill
supplies the missing values from long\-standing convention:
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
background and foreground fall back to color0 and color7
.IP \(bu 3
a missing bright slot repeats its normal counterpart
.IP \(bu 3
the cursor takes the foreground, and the glyph beneath it the background
.IP \(bu 3
selection is inverse video
.RE
.PP
Dim colors are never derived. A dim variant is a darkened color rather than a
copy, and guessing one is worse than leaving it to the terminal.
.PP
Each derived value is annotated in the output where the format permits a
comment, so it cannot be mistaken for one the source stated. Formats without
inline comments annotate on the preceding line; JSON and property lists cannot
be annotated at all, and their derived values are reported on standard error.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The file could not be read, its format could not be identified, the requested
format is unknown, or an editor theme was refused for an incomplete palette.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was invalid.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Convert a konsole scheme to kitty, keeping only what konsole stated:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cscx convert ~/.local/share/konsole/Gruvbox.colorscheme \-\-to kitty
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same, with the cursor and selection colors konsole lacks derived from
convention:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cscx convert Gruvbox.colorscheme \-\-to kitty \-\-fill
.EE
.RE
.PP
Generate a neovim theme from the kitty config you already use:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cscx convert ~/.config/kitty/kitty.conf \-\-to neovim \e
    \-o ~/.config/nvim/colors/mine.lua
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write every supported format and editor into one directory:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cscx convert kitty.conf \-\-to all \-o ./out
.EE
.RE
.PP
Inspect a scheme, or ask what a file is when detection guesses wrong:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cscx parse theme.itermcolors
cscx detect mystery.conf
cscx convert mystery.conf \-\-from ghostty \-\-to alacritty
.EE
.RE
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/kitty/kitty.conf
.TQ
.I ~/.config/alacritty/alacritty.toml
.TQ
.I ~/.config/ghostty/config
.TQ
.I ~/.config/foot/foot.ini
.TQ
.I ~/.local/share/konsole/*.colorscheme
Typical locations of the configs
.B cscx
reads.
.
.SH CAVEATS
Conversion is lossy toward formats with fewer concepts. konsole stores no
cursor or selection color; Windows Terminal has no under\-cursor or
selection\-foreground key; X resources has a cursor color but no under\-cursor
foreground; iTerm2, konsole and Windows Terminal hold no palette entries above
index 15. Values that cannot be carried are dropped, never approximated.
.PP
Some formats cannot express a partial value. wezterm's
.I ansi
array is fixed\-length, so a source missing one normal slot yields a comment
naming the gap rather than an array padded with the wrong colors; foot writes
its cursor as a pair and cannot set one half alone.
.
